---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import buildStats from '../data/build-stats.json';

const title = "Site Build Stats";
const description = "What your browser actually downloads from this site.";

// Format a byte count into human-readable units (binary KB/MB).
const formatBytes = (bytes) => {
  if (!bytes) return '0 B';
  if (bytes < 1024) return `${bytes} B`;
  if (bytes < 1024 * 1024) return `${(bytes / 1024).toFixed(1)} KB`;
  return `${(bytes / (1024 * 1024)).toFixed(2)} MB`;
};

const formatDate = (iso) =>
  new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
    year: 'numeric',
    month: 'long',
    day: 'numeric',
  }).format(new Date(iso));

const { totalSizes, routeAssets, postCount, fileCount, buildTimestamp, gitCommitSha } = buildStats;
const inlinedCss = totalSizes.inlinedCss; // added 2026-05; may be absent in older builds
const searchAssets = buildStats.searchAssets; // added 2026-09; may be absent in older builds
const shortSha = gitCommitSha ? gitCommitSha.slice(0, 7) : null;

// By-type rows for the main breakdown. The literal `css` bucket is omitted
// because Astro inlines CSS into <style> tags, so it always reads 0; the real
// number is surfaced separately as inlinedCss below.
const typeLabels = { html: 'HTML', js: 'JavaScript', images: 'Images', other: 'Other' };
const typeRows = Object.entries(typeLabels)
  .map(([key, label]) => ({ label, ...(totalSizes[key] || { raw: 0, gzipped: 0 }) }))
  .filter((row) => row.raw > 0);

// Per-route rows, largest gzipped footprint first.
const routeRows = Object.entries(routeAssets)
  .map(([route, stats]) => ({ route, ...stats.total }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.gzipped - a.gzipped);

// The homepage is the heaviest real page (both islands live there) and
// /saved-on-hosting the lightest; quoting the pair frames the whole range.
const homeWeight = routeAssets['/']?.total.gzipped;
const staticWeight = routeAssets['/saved-on-hosting']?.total.gzipped;

// Search assets, grouped by when a browser actually asks for them.
const searchRows = searchAssets
  ? [
      {
        label: 'On page load',
        detail: 'the search widget, wherever the box appears',
        ...searchAssets.onPageLoad,
      },
      {
        label: 'On your first search',
        detail: 'the engine and this site\'s language index',
        ...searchAssets.onFirstQuery,
      },
      {
        label: 'Per query',
        detail: `index and content chunks, a few of the ${searchAssets.perQuery.fileCount} at a time`,
        ...searchAssets.perQuery,
      },
    ]
  : [];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <article>
    <h1 class="headingXl">{title}</h1>
    <section>
      <p>
        Large language models make it cheap to build software fitted exactly to
        the job at hand. Widely used frameworks are great, but when you don't
        need all their features, dropping down to something simpler is now easy.
        So in 2026, I migrated the site off Next.js onto Astro. It's still completely 
        static with no server side rendering, nothing computed per request, but 
        also now no unnecessary middleware. This also makes everything quite
        small: the fully static pages carry no JavaScript at all, and what
        weight the site does have is almost entirely the two interactive
        widgets. Everything below measures what a browser actually downloads,
        and is regenerated on every build.
      </p>

      <p class="hero">
        Landing on the homepage downloads <strong>{formatBytes(homeWeight)}</strong>{' '}
        gzipped. A static page here is <strong>{formatBytes(staticWeight)}</strong>.
      </p>

      <p class="lightText meta">
        {postCount} blog posts · built {formatDate(buildTimestamp)}
        {shortSha && (
          <> · commit <a href={`https://github.com/mjelgart/melgart.net/commit/${gitCommitSha}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{shortSha}</a></>
        )}
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="headingLg">By file type</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Type</th><th>Gzipped</th><th>Raw</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {typeRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.label}</td>
              <td>{formatBytes(row.gzipped)}</td>
              <td class="lightText">{formatBytes(row.raw)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          <tr class="totalRow">
            <td>Total</td>
            <td>{formatBytes(totalSizes.total.gzipped)}</td>
            <td class="lightText">{formatBytes(totalSizes.total.raw)}</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {inlinedCss && (
        <p class="lightText note">
          CSS is inlined into the HTML (no separate stylesheet requests):
          {' '}{formatBytes(inlinedCss.gzipped)} gzipped, already counted within HTML above.
          These totals cover the site's own {fileCount} files; the search bundle
          is measured separately below.
        </p>
      )}
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="headingLg">By page</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Route</th><th>Gzipped</th><th>Raw</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {routeRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.route}</td>
              <td>{formatBytes(row.gzipped)}</td>
              <td class="lightText">{formatBytes(row.raw)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="lightText note">
        Each page counts only the HTML and JavaScript it actually loads, so
        fully static pages stay tiny; the pages with interactive islands
        (<code>/</code>, <code>/sports</code>) also pull a shared React bundle,
        and <code>/</code> the search widget on top of it. Images aren't
        included here; they're in the site total above. <code>/posts</code>
        rolls up all blog posts rather than counting as a single page.
      </p>
    </section>

    {searchAssets && (
      <section>
        <h2 class="headingLg">Search</h2>
        <p>
          Search is <a href="https://pagefind.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pagefind</a>,
          which is static too: there's no search server, just an index the
          browser downloads a piece at a time. Almost none of it loads unless
          you actually search.
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>When</th><th>Gzipped</th><th>Raw</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {searchRows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{row.label}<br /><span class="lightText detail">{row.detail}</span></td>
                <td>{formatBytes(row.gzipped)}</td>
                <td class="lightText">{formatBytes(row.raw)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="lightText note">
          The index and language payloads are already compressed on disk, so the
          gzipped column barely moves for them — and for the chunks it reads
          slightly larger, which is gzip adding framing to data it can't shrink.
        </p>
        <p class="lightText note">
          Pagefind also ships {formatBytes(searchAssets.neverFetched.raw)} across
          {' '}{searchAssets.neverFetched.fileCount} files that nothing on this site
          ever requests — two alternative search UIs, a result-highlighting script,
          and a fallback language index. It emits the whole set on every build
          regardless of which one you use. It costs nothing to leave there: bytes
          nobody fetches are bytes nobody pays for.
        </p>
      </section>
    )}
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .hero {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .meta {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.75rem 0.4rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  th + th,
  td + td {
    text-align: right;
  }

  .totalRow td {
    border-top: 2px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-bottom: none;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .note {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .detail {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
</style>
